Having ascended twice now in nh3.1 (once as a Samurai, which in my opinion is
now just about the EASIEST class, and once as a tourist) I've got a few
ideas which I'd like to share.

We all have our own playing styles (I for instance, try to get displacement,
stealth and speed as fast as I can...), but reflection seems to be almost
a necessity early on now, especially in the mines. However, increasing
the numbers of amulets/shields of reflection would be unbalacing, to say
the least. Sooooo, mirrors seem to be pretty easy to find, how about being
able to (W)ear them like a shield? They would have a (small/medium/large?)
chance of breaking in combat (depending on blessed status?) possibly doing
(small) damage when they broke. They would add nothing (+0, nada) to AC
but would give you the reflection intrinsic. Nymphs, of course, would behave
the same way as they do now when you (a)pply the mirror at them. Since mirrors
are rather heavy, I don't think this would be too unbalancing (so to speak).
What about luck effects?

Secondly, let me add my voice to the chorus of the dissatisfied with the 
game balace later on. By the time I've reached the Castle in both of my
ascentions, I knew I couldn't fail. Dragons and liches gave me almost no pause,
and I crused through hell. In fact, The game got plain boring from the time
I got the amulet to the time I got onto the last plane (even the elemental
planes were fairly simple, if not tedious). Soooo, as has been suggested
by others: A) Give monsters wands of cancelation - wands of death really
aren't effective at all due to luck, magic resistence and refection at this
point. Seeing your AC going from -30 to -2 would give some people pause
though. B) Have a "luck stealer" in the game (like gremlins). What with
a guaranteed luck stone now, this makes for super-charaters later on.
Gremlins or leprachauns (say) that stole your luck would make the game
rather more difficult. C) I agree that the "bigguns" should be more 
impressive. Dragons are far too whimpy. Perhaps there should be older
(and larger) dragons further down. How about Beholders (`a la AD&D) for
really scary eye-guys?

The other option for improving the end game would be to implement Dant'e's
Hell. Put a Duke of Hell (with attached castle/lair/cesspool) on each level
(like Vlad's castle, the Wizard's tower etc..) and make each one somewhat
different. There could be a "mother of all dragons" level for instance...
The Wizard would be at the bottom with the amulet, of course. The
bell, book and candelabra were used by Faust to enter hell, rather than
the way it is currently done IN hell. Thus, the Quest, the Gorgon and
perhaps the Minotaur (the father of all minotaurs) could have the three
components. Or perhaps a master lich in the castle? Anyway, I would
guarantee a couple of maze levels between the Gorgon and the Castle
(more than this just gets REALLY tedious) and make the 9 circles of 
hell the final levels below the Castle.

Also, the Astral plane should be tougher. I found it a little easy
going at the moment. Beef up the Demi-Gods? How about Avatars of the Gods?
You would have to kill/avoid the other two non-aligned Gods (who would
have intrinsic lightning/fire/cold attacks) and make it to your own 
peaceful temple. Note that Avatars are aspects of Gods, not Gods themselves -
i.e. they should be tough, but killable.

Finally some aspects of the game I consider to be so unbalancing as to
be bugs...

- The "bell abuse" is so bad, this has to be considered a bug.
  This is worse than kinking sinks or polypiling ever were.
- The tourist (and I believe the priest) quests need to be re-thought,
  for the same reason. If you don't know what I'm talking about play
  them and see. I loved the tourist quest though. Great concept.
- Some of the quest artifacts are overpowering, like the Tsurgi of Muramasa
  and the Platinum Yendorian Express card. Maybe thay could be toned down
  a bit. You definitely should not be able to wish for _any_ quest item.
- Polypiling is still a little powerful, mostly because of Fort Ludios.
  How can one not resist zaping that little ol' polymorph wand at those
  piles of stuff that go on for three pages each.... I've always had
  great armour by the time I leave.

Well, thats <more than> enough for now. Flame away.
